# Line endings are a correctness issue here, not a style one.
#
# This repo is developed on Windows. A shell script committed with CRLF fails
# on Linux with `bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M`, and a Makefile with
# CRLF fails with equally unhelpful errors -- which would break the Linux CI
# on its first run and look like a platform bug rather than a checkout bug.
#
# Normalise everything textual to LF in the repository. Working copies on
# Windows are unaffected for editing purposes.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.sh    text eol=lf
*.py    text eol=lf
*.c     text eol=lf
*.h     text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.toml  text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.in    text eol=lf
*.jsonl text eol=lf
Makefile text eol=lf
LICENSE  text eol=lf

# Never touched.
#
# *.genna especially: python/tests/fixtures/format_v3.genna is the fixture
# that pins the on-disk format. Under `* text=auto` git decides byte-by-byte
# whether a file looks textual, and it currently guesses binary for this one
# -- but that is a heuristic about the fixture's *contents*, not a rule. A
# future fixture over more text-like data would be line-ending converted on
# checkout, corrupting the one file whose whole job is to be byte-exact, and
# it would fail only on Linux and macOS. Say it explicitly instead.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.ply   binary
*.tar   binary
*.gz    binary
*.gn    binary
*.genna binary
*.wal   binary
